<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Statistical anomaly analysis</title>
<style>body{font:16px/1.55 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#202f3b;max-width:950px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem}h1,h2{color:#225977}section{margin:3rem 0;border-top:1px solid #cad7dd;padding-top:1rem}img{max-width:100%}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border-bottom:1px solid #dce3e8;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.note{background:#eaf2f5;padding:1rem}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;overflow-wrap:anywhere}</style></head><body>
<h1>Statistical anomaly analysis</h1><p>{{ metadata.dataset_name }} · {{ metadata.timestamp }}</p>
<p class="note">Results indicate statistical anomalies, not proof of wrongdoing. The index is relative, not a probability.</p>
<h2>Anomaly index: {{ index if index is not none else 'Not available' }}{% if index is not none %} / 100{% endif %}</h2><p>Components: {{ components }}</p>{% if scoring_warning %}<p class="note">Anomaly scoring not available: {{ scoring_warning }}</p>{% endif %}
<img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ cover }}" alt="Analysis dashboard">
<h2>Executive summary</h2>{% for item in summary %}<p><strong>{{ item.severity }}</strong> {{ item.statement }}</p>{% endfor %}
<section><h2>Dataset profile and applicability</h2><p>{{ profile.rows }} rows · {{ profile.columns }} columns. There is no fixed minimum sample size: fewer eligible observations mean wider sampling uncertainty; results from a narrow value range may not be representative.</p>
{% for name, col in profile.column_profiles.items() %}<p>{{ name }}: {{ col.dtype }}, {{ col.missing }} missing; {{ col.finite_count }} finite numeric values, {{ col.positive_count }} positive values. Finite range: {{ col.finite_min }} to {{ col.finite_max }}; positive range: {{ col.positive_min }} to {{ col.positive_max }} ({{ col.positive_orders_span }} orders of magnitude). Conservative 95% ECDF uncertainty bound (DKW, for independent observations): ±{{ col.ecdf_95_band }}. Digit suitability {{ col.benford }} ({{ col.benford_reasons|join('; ') }}); continuous suitability {{ col.continuous }} ({{ col.continuous_reasons|join('; ') }}).</p>{% endfor %}</section>
{% for section in sections %}<section><h2>{{ section.title }}</h2><h3>NUMBERS</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for key,value in section.numbers.items() %}<tr><td>{{ key }}</td><td>{{ value }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>
{% if section.numbers.bins is defined %}<h4>Per-digit values</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Digit</th><th>Observed</th><th>Expected</th><th>z</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for digit in section.numbers.bins %}<tr><td>{{ digit }}</td><td>{{ section.numbers.observed[loop.index0] }}</td><td>{{ section.numbers.expected[loop.index0] }}</td><td>{{ section.numbers.z_scores[loop.index0] }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table>{% endif %}
<h3>CHART</h3>{% for name,image,caption in section.charts %}<figure><img src="data:image/png;base64,{{ image }}" alt="{{ name }} chart"><figcaption>How to read this: {{ caption }}</figcaption></figure>{% endfor %}
<h3>STATISTICAL READING</h3>{% for text in section.reading %}<p>{{ text }}</p>{% endfor %}{% for text in section.caveats %}<p>{{ text }}</p>{% endfor %}{% for text in section.warnings %}<p>{{ text }}</p>{% endfor %}</section>{% endfor %}
<section><h2>Flagged-records appendix (zero-based positions)</h2><p>{{ flagged_rows }}</p></section>
<section><h2>Methodology and limitations</h2><p>No fixed row-count cutoff determines eligibility. For n finite independent observations the conservative 95% Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz ECDF bound is min(1, sqrt(log(40)/(2n))). This is an uncertainty guide, not a p-value for any fitted reference; small or dependent samples need extra care. Positive value ranges are reported as log10(max) − log10(min), without a range threshold. Benford chi-square calibration is unreliable if expected bin counts fall below five; those results are marked with a warning, not suppressed. Benford MAD uses Nigrini descriptive thresholds; KS D and Kuiper V use descriptive scales of 0.2 and 0.3, not universal critical values. P-values use asymptotic or Monte Carlo methods as marked; valid p-values receive Benjamini-Hochberg adjustment. Isolation Forest is unsupervised: record scores rank within this dataset and do not represent probabilities. Index = 100 × (0.2 × review share + 0.5 × normalized distribution effect + 0.3 × adjusted significance).</p></section>
<section><h2>Reproducibility</h2><pre>{{ metadata }}</pre></section></body></html>
